\title{Formula Interface of the Estimation and Testing Functions}
\description{
The estimation and testing functions and \code{\link{kmcurve_fast}} take
vectors of times, event indicators, and group labels, which suits repeated
calls inside a simulation loop. For the analysis of a single data set they
also accept a model formula with a \code{Surv(time, event)} response and a
data frame, as the functions of the \pkg{survival} package do:

\preformatted{
coxph_fast(Surv(time, status) ~ arm, data = d)
survdiff_fast(Surv(time, status) ~ arm + strata(region), data = d)
rmst_fast(Surv(time, status) ~ 1, data = d, tau = 12)
}

The formula is given as the first argument (by position, or by the name of
that argument, such as \code{time}; there is no argument named
\code{formula}), and the data frame as \code{data} or as the second
argument. The other arguments (\code{side},
\code{tau}, \code{conf.level}, and so on) are given by name as with the
vector interface, and the result is the same as that of the vector
interface with the vectors evaluated from the formula.
}
\details{
The response must be \code{Surv(time, event)} with right-censored data,
where \code{event} is 1 (or \code{TRUE}) for an event and 0 (or
\code{FALSE}) for a censored time; an expression such as
\code{Surv(time, status == 2)} may be used. A status coded 1 (censored)
and 2 (event), as in data sets of the \pkg{survival} package, gives an
error that suggests this expression. The call to \code{Surv()} is
read from the formula and not evaluated, so the \pkg{survival} package
need not be attached. Other forms of \code{Surv()}, such as counting-process
data, are not supported.

The right-hand side is \code{1} for a single group, or a single group
variable with exactly two distinct values for a two-group comparison. Which
of the two is allowed follows the vector interface of each function:
\code{\link{survfit_fast}} takes only \code{~ 1}; \code{\link{rmst_fast}},
\code{\link{wmst_fast}}, \code{\link{medsurv_fast}}, and
\code{\link{kmcurve_fast}} take \code{~ 1} or a group variable; the other
functions require a group variable. \code{\link{survdiff_fast}} and
\code{\link{coxph_fast}} also accept \code{strata()} terms, such as
\code{arm + strata(region)} or \code{arm + strata(region, sex)}; several
stratification variables define a stratum for each combination of their
values. Interactions, further covariates, and other formula operators are
not supported, because the functions compare two groups without
adjustment.

With a group variable, \code{control} defaults to its first level: the
first level of a factor that occurs in the data, and otherwise the smallest
value. This is the reference level of a model formula with the group
variable as a factor. \code{control} can be given to choose the other group
as the control group. A numeric group variable is treated as two groups,
whereas \code{survival::coxph()} treats it as a continuous covariate, so
the coefficient of \code{survival::coxph()} equals the log hazard ratio of
the two groups only when the two values differ by 1.

The variables are evaluated in \code{data}, and variables not found there in
the environment of the formula. Missing values in the variables of the
formula are an error, as in the vector interface; remove the incomplete rows
first, for example with \code{na.omit()}.

The formula interface adds the cost of evaluating the formula to every call,
so the vector interface, with \code{presorted = TRUE} where available,
remains the choice inside simulation loops.
}
\examples{
library(survival)

# The same hazard ratio from the formula and from the vectors
coxph_fast(Surv(futime, fustat) ~ rx, data = ovarian)
coxph_fast(ovarian$futime, ovarian$fustat, ovarian$rx, control = 1)

# Stratified log-rank test
survdiff_fast(Surv(futime, fustat) ~ rx + strata(resid.ds), data = ovarian)

# Single-group and two-group restricted mean survival time
rmst_fast(Surv(futime, fustat) ~ 1, data = ovarian, tau = 600)
rmst_fast(Surv(futime, fustat) ~ rx, data = ovarian, tau = 600, control = 2)
}
